  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nSunny skies will return today as high pressure builds into central \nIL. Strong warm advection building this ridge will result in \nhighs surging into the mid 80s today, just a few degrees from \nrecord values, and into the lower 90s Monday, exceeding previous\nrecord highs. A weak cold front will push into the area \nTuesday, bringing some minor cooling to areas north of I-72 and \na slight chance for showers or thunderstorms.\n\nModels are in good agreement that precipitation will develop Tuesday \nnight as the stalled frontal zone strengthens, a low level jet \ndevelops, and increased gulf moisture arrives. 60 to 80 percent PoPs \nare included in the current forecast package Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning. Precipitation amounts remain uncertain, but 00Z \nGFS advertises amounts over an inch in much of central IL, while 12Z \nECMWF and Canadian models show generally under a half inch. Several \nhundred J/kg MUCAPE and 30-40 kt bulk shear appear likely Tuesday \nevening in a corridor extending from northern MO into west-central \nIL as 12Z ensemble depicts 60+ percent chances for joint probability \nof shear greater than 30 kts and MUCAPE greater than 500 J/kg, which \nwould support potential for severe storms. SPC has designated a \nMarginal Risk for severe storms in this area.\n\nLater in the week, a central Rockies low looks to eject eastward and \nmerge with a Canadian low to bring another 70 percent chance for \nshowers and thunderstorms Thursday.\n\nColder air following Thursday's system will arrive for the weekend, \nhowever the trajectory of the coldest air remains uncertain, with \nmodels varying considerably. For central IL, 12Z ensembles have \naround a 40 degree spread for Saturday afternoon. Current forecast \nreflects roughly a median value, with highs in the upper 50s and \nlows in the upper 30s Saturday/Saturday night, but a potential for \nfreezing temperatures or frost will have to be monitored for this \ntime period.",
